'''''Asteriscus aquaticus''''' is a species of [[flowering plant]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Flann |first1=Christina |title=Asteriscus aquaticus (L.) Less. |url=https://compositae.landcareresearch.co.nz/default.aspx?Page=NameDetails&TabNum=0&NameId=0da7ec7f-a893-4db1-957b-b8f605df7de6 |work=Global Compositae Checklist |access-date=28 December 2019 |language=en}}</ref> The flower is part of the so-called "''[[Asteriscus (plant)|Asteriscus]]'' alliance".<ref>{{harvp|Goertzen|2002|p=818}}.</ref>

Formally known by its [[basionym]], '''''Bupthalmum aquaticum''''', when it was originally described in 1753 as a species of the ''[[Buphthalmum]]'' genus.<ref>{{cite web |title=Name – Buphthalmum aquaticum L. |url=http://www.tropicos.org/Name/50103508 |work=Tropicos |access-date=28 December 2019 |language=en |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191228183007/http://www.tropicos.org/Name/50103508 |archive-date=28 December 2019}}</ref> Its original name meant '''sweet-scented ox eye'''.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Clarke |first1=Stephen Reynolds |title=Hortus Anglicus: Or, The Modern English Garden: Containing a Familiar Description of All the Plants which are Cultivated in the Climate of Great Britain, Either for Use Or Ornament, and of a Selection from the Established Favourites of the Stove and Green-house; Arranged According to the System of Linnaeus; with Remarks on the Properties of the More Valuable Species |date=1822 |publisher=F. C. & J. Rivington |page=411 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=toBIAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA411 |access-date=28 December 2019 |language=en}}</ref> The plant is a low-growing [[Annual vs. perennial plant evolution|annual]] herb with orange-yellow flowers, native to the Mediterranean region. It flowers between April and June and colonizes dry coastal areas.<ref>{{cite book |last=Lanfranco |first=Edwin |title=Wild flowers of the Maltese Islands |date=2015 |others=Guido Bonett |isbn=978-99957-46-59-9 |location=San Gwann |oclc=992500794}}</ref>

== See also ==
* ''[[Buphthalmum salicifolium]]'' – one of two species still found within the ''Buphthalmum'' genus.
* ''[[Pallenis maritima]]'' – a closely related species.

